{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe over-optimism: The model consistently forecasts a slow, linear upward drift (approx. +0.10-0.15 EUR/day) regardless of recent price spikes, failing to anticipate the sharp mean reversion observed in the true data.",
    "Implement immediate downward correction after sentiment spikes: When sentiment history shows a spike (1.00) or high positive sentiment, the model fails to price in the subsequent 10-15 day crash; apply a -3 to -5 EUR correction to the first 10 forecast days.",
    "Adjust for lag in volatility: The model ignores the high-frequency volatility (swing of ~10 EUR) seen in the true outcomes; forecasts should be adjusted to reflect a wider confidence interval rather than a tight trend line.",
    "Horizon-specific bias: The model's error grows exponentially with the forecast horizon; for horizons beyond 10 days, the forecast is systematically too high by 5-10 EUR due to the assumption of continued growth.",
    "Sentiment divergence: A negative sentiment reading (-0.33) in the history correlates with the start of the price decline, yet the model continues to project increases; trigger a regime switch to a bearish mean-reversion model when sentiment drops below 0."
  ]
}
